               <code>
                  <coding>
                     <system value="http://loinc.org"/>
                     <code value="8648-8"/>
                     <display value="Hospital course note"/>
                  </coding>
               </code>
               <text>
                  <status value="generated"/>
                  <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the recent hospitalisation of your patient, a 61-year-old male, admitted on 13th September 1975 with acute right lower quadrant abdominal pain, nausea, and low-grade fever, clinically consistent with acute appendicitis. On admission, physical examination revealed marked tenderness at McBurney's point. Blood tests confirmed leukocytosis. Abdominal ultrasound supported the diagnosis of acute appendicitis without evidence of perforation. The patient underwent laparoscopic appendectomy under general anaesthesia, which proceeded without complications. Postoperatively, intravenous fluids and analgesics were administered. Wound care was performed regularly. Vital signs remained stable throughout admission. The patient recovered well and was discharged on 17th September 1975 in good general condition. Pathological examination confirmed acute appendicitis. Please ensure appropriate outpatient wound follow-up. Do not hesitate to contact me should any questions arise.</div>
               </text>
            </section>
